hkA:hi Because I am in my economics class and that I don't want to 
   do the job the teacher asked us to do under Micro$oft Excel !

hmQ:he Tell me about Heroes' history...
hkA:hi Well, we started this game in summer 96. The idea came form Guen.
   At this moment, we were only three: Guen, Toalnkor (now known as Tnk)
   and me (Pollux); Alexel joimed us one year later.  The development was
   going quite slow (it took us two years actualy) mainly because of our
   studies. Maybe the fact that Guen and me communicated by (snail-)mail
   was another slowing down factor. I sent more than 60 letters to Guen
   during this two days !  During the first year, we decided to sale the
   game.  We made a demo version (with only 10 levels, no editor, and an
   order form), and a CD version (with songs remixed on audio track, but
   not used in the game). Our main issue was the price of Midas' licence;
   we were also hesitating on the way we should distribute Heroes
   (shall we di it shareware or comercial ?).  Finaly, after a couple of
   wasted months we decided to spread it freeware (Enjoy !), because we
   think that no one would realy like to buy such a game today; today
   where 3D rules the (P)sea.
   Now let's talk about the content of the game.  At first, Tnk decided
   to make some four channels songs in the old original mod format.
   As there were only two scores done and we were waiting for the next
   tunes, we used some songs from Clawz just to vary. These were 'overtaking'
   and 'hibakusha2' and gave a better taste to the game.  So we removed old
   Tnk's songs and he was asked to make some more Clawz-like musics. By the
   time, Alexel joined and came with 10 channels XM, thus all tunes are now
   8 or 10 channels XM... There are 13 tunes in the game (for a total of
   2.88Mb unpacked), these are the introtune, the menutune, the 10 levels,
   and the endtune (you can't hear the later without finishing the quest
   game); most of them have been done by Alexel.
   All sprites and pictures were drawn by Guen.  He first made some
   level-tiles and a set of hand-done previews of the game befire any
   other work get started (I guess he has a few other projects at this
   state of devlopment at home).  These screens and tiles were used to
   make the level editor.  Actualy I learned C while making this editor
   so there is some piece of code I am not proud of into (and also in the
   game).  When it was nearly done, I started to code the game.  The
   first thing done was the introduction, and then came the multi-
   directionnal scroller.  I remember me that the first level that were
   loaded was sized 4x4 and wrapped in all directions; this was very small
   but at this time there were no tail and the vehicles were crossings the
   walls...  There are funny things to know about some peace of code.
   A friend of mine (Renaud Guerin) said I should write it there. There
   is places in the game where you see some text on a moving blue logo
   (the first screen [may be disabled] and the end scroller), this
   movment is using some polynomial aproximation of triangular functions.
   I only wanted to try this, and it showed that I couldn't use this to
   draw a simple circle.  But wath I am quite proud of is the pendulum
   effect you sea when you start a level (with one player) : this is a
   real pendulum simulation with air resistance (not a fake with
   some bare sin*exp properly scaled), and I used Euler's algorithm to
   approximate the solution of this unsolvable differential equation.
   I am quite pleased about the result.
   Most of the levels were done by Guen. But I and Tnk also did some.
   Guen made a cleanup of all the level we made : some were relegated in
   the EXTRALVL directory, other were simply erased. We aimed to spread
   the game with 100 of them, but as it is going late and that we are not
   going to sale the game anymore I don't know how less levels there will be
   at the time I am writting this.


